    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "adjList = [[2,4],[1,3],[2,4],[1,3]]   ->  [[2,4],[1,3],[2,4],[1,3]]\n                                          (same shape, all new nodes)\nadjList = [[]]                        ->  [[]]     (one node, no neighbours)\nadjList = []                          ->  []       (no graph: null in, null out)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "`adjList[i]` lists the neighbours of the node with value `i + 1`; you're handed the node with value 1."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea: walk the graph, and remember each copy"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Walk every node with DFS or BFS. When you first meet an original node, create its copy. Wire each copy's `neighbors` to the **copies** of the original's neighbours."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The trap is **cycles**: node 1's neighbour 2 has neighbour 1 again. A hash map from **original → copy** solves both problems at once — it's the visited set (a node in the map has been met), and it's how you find the copy to wire to. Put each copy in the map **before** walking its neighbours, so a cycle that leads back finds it there instead of copying forever."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. DFS with a map of copies"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function cloneGraph(node) {\n  if (node === null) return null;\n  const copies = new Map();               // original node -> its copy\n  const clone = (orig) => {\n    if (copies.has(orig)) return copies.get(orig);   // met before (maybe round a cycle): reuse it\n    const copy = new Node(orig.val);\n    copies.set(orig, copy);               // record it BEFORE the neighbours, so cycles stop here\n    for (const nb of orig.neighbors) copy.neighbors.push(clone(nb));\n    return copy;\n  };\n  return clone(node);\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def clone_graph(node):\n    if node is None:\n        return None\n    copies = {}                           # original node -> its copy\n\n    def clone(orig):\n        if orig in copies:\n            return copies[orig]           # met before (maybe round a cycle): reuse it\n        copy = Node(orig.val)\n        copies[orig] = copy               # record it BEFORE the neighbours, so cycles stop here\n        for nb in orig.neighbors:\n            copy.neighbors.append(clone(nb))\n        return copy\n\n    return clone(node)"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "Map<Node, Node> copies = new HashMap<>();   // original node -> its copy\n\nNode cloneGraph(Node node) {\n    if (node == null) return null;\n    if (copies.containsKey(node)) return copies.get(node);   // met before: reuse it\n    Node copy = new Node(node.val);\n    copies.put(node, copy);               // record it BEFORE the neighbours, so cycles stop here\n    for (Node nb : node.neighbors) copy.neighbors.add(cloneGraph(nb));\n    return copy;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "unordered_map<Node*, Node*> copies;       // original node -> its copy\n\nNode* cloneGraph(Node* node) {\n    if (node == nullptr) return nullptr;\n    if (copies.count(node)) return copies[node];   // met before: reuse it\n    Node* copy = new Node(node->val);\n    copies[node] = copy;                  // record it BEFORE the neighbours, so cycles stop here\n    for (Node* nb : node->neighbors) copy->neighbors.push_back(cloneGraph(nb));\n    return copy;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(V + E), space O(V).** Each node is copied once and each edge is wired once from each end. The map holds V entries, and the recursion can be V deep."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. BFS with a map of copies"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The same map, filled by a queue. Create a node's copy the first time you **see** it as a neighbour, and add it to the queue; every edge you look at becomes one wire between two copies."
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "clone-graph",
      "caption": "The copy grows beside the original. Each edge looked at becomes one wire between two copies."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function cloneGraph(node) {\n  if (node === null) return null;\n  const copies = new Map([[node, new Node(node.val)]]);   // original -> copy\n  const queue = [node];\n  let head = 0;\n  while (head < queue.length) {\n    const orig = queue[head++];\n    for (const nb of orig.neighbors) {\n      if (!copies.has(nb)) {\n        copies.set(nb, new Node(nb.val)); // first time we see nb: make its copy\n        queue.push(nb);\n      }\n      copies.get(orig).neighbors.push(copies.get(nb));   // wire the copied edge\n    }\n  }\n  return copies.get(node);\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "from collections import deque\n\ndef clone_graph(node):\n    if node is None:\n        return None\n    copies = {node: Node(node.val)}       # original -> copy\n    queue = deque([node])\n    while queue:\n        orig = queue.popleft()\n        for nb in orig.neighbors:\n            if nb not in copies:\n                copies[nb] = Node(nb.val) # first time we see nb: make its copy\n                queue.append(nb)\n            copies[orig].neighbors.append(copies[nb])   # wire the copied edge\n    return copies[node]"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "Node cloneGraph(Node node) {\n    if (node == null) return null;\n    Map<Node, Node> copies = new HashMap<>();\n    copies.put(node, new Node(node.val)); // original -> copy\n    ArrayDeque<Node> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    queue.add(node);\n    while (!queue.isEmpty()) {\n        Node orig = queue.poll();\n        for (Node nb : orig.neighbors) {\n            if (!copies.containsKey(nb)) {\n                copies.put(nb, new Node(nb.val));   // first time we see nb: make its copy\n                queue.add(nb);\n            }\n            copies.get(orig).neighbors.add(copies.get(nb));   // wire the copied edge\n        }\n    }\n    return copies.get(node);\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "Node* cloneGraph(Node* node) {\n    if (node == nullptr) return nullptr;\n    unordered_map<Node*, Node*> copies;\n    copies[node] = new Node(node->val);   // original -> copy\n    queue<Node*> q;\n    q.push(node);\n    while (!q.empty()) {\n        Node* orig = q.front(); q.pop();\n        for (Node* nb : orig->neighbors) {\n            if (!copies.count(nb)) {\n                copies[nb] = new Node(nb->val);   // first time we see nb: make its copy\n                q.push(nb);\n            }\n            copies[orig]->neighbors.push_back(copies[nb]);   // wire the copied edge\n        }\n    }\n    return copies[node];\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(V + E), space O(V).** The same work, without recursion."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "DFS with a map of copies",
          "time": "O(V + E)",
          "timeWhy": "Each node is copied **once**, and each edge wired once from each end.",
          "space": "O(V)",
          "spaceWhy": "The map holds **V** copies; the recursion can be V deep."
        },
        {
          "approach": "BFS with a map of copies",
          "time": "O(V + E)",
          "timeWhy": "The **same work**: each node queued once, each edge wired once.",
          "space": "O(V)",
          "spaceWhy": "The map and the queue, up to **V** nodes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Null input** — return null.",
        "**One node, no neighbours** — one new node with an empty list.",
        "**Two nodes pointing at each other** — the smallest cycle; the map must stop the recursion.",
        "**Neighbour order** — copy the neighbours in the same order as the original."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Copy List with Random Pointer** — the same original → copy map, on a linked list whose extra pointer can point anywhere.",
        "**Clone a binary tree with random pointers** — walk the tree, map each original node to its copy, then wire the random pointers.",
        "**Clone N-ary Tree** — no cycles, so no map is needed: plain recursion."
      ]
    }
